<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alberico da Romano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1196–<a href="/years/1260">1260</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alberico da Romano was lord of Treviso and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano">Ezzelino III da Romano</a>, sharing in the family's domination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-treviso">March of Treviso</a>. He ruled Treviso harshly for two decades. After Ezzelino's defeat and death in 1259, Alberico was besieged at San Zenone and killed with his family in 1260.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alberico, called Alberico II, was born in the castle of San Zenone to Ezzelino II da Romano and Adelaide Alberti di Mangona; his siblings included Ezzelino III and Cunizza. With his first wife, Beatrice, a noblewoman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, he had a daughter, Adelaide, who married Rinaldo d'Este in 1235, and five sons, one of whom, Ezzelino, was killed in battle in 1243. With his second wife, Margherita, he had three daughters, Griselda, Tornalisce and Amabilia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Alberico was a condottiero who was by turns a Guelph and a Ghibelline. Allied with his brother, he served as podestà of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> for the Emperor Frederick II in 1227. In 1239 he broke with the Ghibelline faction, allied with the Guelph Guecellone da Camino and helped the Milanese against the emperor. In 1240 he took Treviso as a Guelph and became its podestà, but he governed it as a Ghibelline until 1257. Pope Alexander IV excommunicated him, and after his brother's death following the Battle of Cassano d'Adda in 1259 he was driven from Treviso and took refuge at San Zenone with most of his family.</p><p>On 25 August 1260 Guelph troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> surrounded the castle, and Alberico surrendered in the hope of saving his family. The next day his sons, some of them young children, were cut to pieces before him, and the women of his family were paraded naked and burned alive. Alberico was tortured with hot irons and dragged through Treviso tied to a horse's tail until he died. The chronicler Salimbene de Adam wrote that he saw these things with his own eyes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Alberico was a patron and friend of troubadours, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sordello">Sordello</a> and Uc de Saint Circ, and wrote Occitan verse himself. A chansonnier produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> in 1254 under his patronage, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biblioteca-estense">Biblioteca Estense</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, contains the Liber Alberici, the Book of Alberic. He composed a tenso with Uc, Mesier Albric, so.m prega Ardisons. The poem Na Maria, pretç e fina valors has been attributed to him, but most scholars today assign it to Bieiris de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alberico da Romano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Ezzelino III da Romano's brother <b>Alberico</b> it suffered harsh rule until 1259.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-treviso">Commune of Treviso</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Zenone degli Ezzelini, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in San Zenone degli Ezzelini, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.78,11.83&amp;year=1237" aria-label="San Zenone degli Ezzelini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4830 550 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alberico da Romano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5280 820h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5280 820h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.78,11.83&amp;year=1237">What was here in 1237? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
